[/asy] (Let $(x,y)$ denote the center of one of the circles, viewed in the system where the sum of the radii is $\sqrt{6}$ and the ellipse is centered at the origin.)

Then from the equation of the ellipse and from the fact that the circle is centered at $(x,y),$ we have \[\begin{aligned} (x^2 + 5(y-r)^2 &= 6 \\ \sqrt{6-x^2} &= y+r.\end{aligned}\]Substituting the second equation into the first one gives us \[x^2 + 5(\sqrt{6-x^2}-r)^2 = 6.\]If $x=0,$ then $(6-x^2)^2$ is maximized, giving us $r=\boxed{0.2}.$ </think> <answer> 0.2 </answer>

